    "https://jobs.boisestate.edu/jobs/lecturer-biology-boise-idaho-united-states": {
      "title": "Lecturer - Biology",
      "description": "Lecturer - Biology - Boise, Idaho, United States Back to Boise State My Profile Kiosk mode Lecturer - Biology Lecturer - Biology 499937 Boise, Idaho Faculty Opening on: Oct 29 2026 The Department of Biological Sciences at Boise State University invites applications for a Lecturer to teach Anatomy and Physiology and related courses. The successful candidate will be appointed at the lecturer rank that best aligns with their qualifications and experience. The anticipated teaching load is 4/4, with courses offered in online and face-to-face formats. The lecturer will primarily teach lecture-based courses, with opportunities to teach laboratory sections. These courses serve students in programs across campus. Approximately 80% of the position will focus on teaching and curriculum coordination. The lecturer will design, develop, and update course materials, lesson plans, curricula, and other instructional resources; deliver courses using a range of teaching methods and platforms; and collaborate with other instructors to align curriculum (e.g., scaffolding across courses or labs). Approximately 20% will focus on assessing student learning and success, using those findings to inform course design and instruction, mentoring and supporting undergraduate students, and completing other activities based on department needs. The successful candidate will bring knowledge of effective teaching practices and the ability to adapt, listen actively, and work effectively both independently and collaboratively. The role calls for professionalism and integrity, and the ability to engage productively with students, colleagues, and department leadership in a dynamic, collaborative environment. Department Overview The Department of Biological Sciences at Boise State University holds a strong commitment to excellence in teaching, research and service. Our department serves over 600 students majoring in biology, and many more students taking biology courses for other degree programs. We share teaching experiences and strategies in order to improve the experiences of our students. Our department is committed to the success of our early career faculty and provides informal and formal mentoring. Our curriculum is designed to support students in developing critical thinking and data analysis skills, laboratory expertise, scientific writing, and oral communication skills. Research in our department spans all levels of biological sciences creating numerous opportunities for collaboration and growth. In addition, we frequently collaborate with faculty, staff members, and students in other departments and colleges for research, teaching and service efforts. Minimum Qualifications Masters degree in Biological Sciences or a closely-related field. Preferred Qualifications An interest in developing and delivering undergraduate curriculum. A commitment to monitoring and improving teaching and thoughtful course design A commitment to creating inclusive teaching environments where all students can succeed. Prior teaching or mentorship experience in the biological sciences. Required Application Materials: Cover letter ~1 page Provide a synopsis of why you are interested in this specific position in the Department of Biological Sciences at Boise State, your relevant experiences, and your qualifications Curriculum vitae that includes: Employment history Relevant teaching and mentorship experience (courses taught, course development, mentorship roles) Professional activities including service and professional development in teaching Teaching Statement (two page maximum) that describes: How you would approach designing a new course, including assessment strategies How you would create a welcoming and engaging classroom environment that supports the success of all learners How you plan to monitor and improve your courses over time Transcripts (unofficial is OK) from all undergraduate and graduate coursework Please also provide contact information for three professional references, including names, phone numbers, and email addresses. Reference information will be entered directly into the application system and does not need to be uploaded as a separate document. Closing Date: To be considered in the first round of review, applications must be received by 11/18/2026. Review will begin on this date, but the position will remain open until filled. For questions regarding this position, please contact Sarah Dalrymple ( sarahdalrymple@boisestate.edu ) or Eric Hayden ( erichayden@boisestate.edu ). Salary and Benefits: The position is open to candidates at all lecturer ranks. The anticipated salary ranges from $48,793 to $56,111 depending on experience. Our standard faculty contract is a 9-month contract (unless otherwise noted) with pay and benefits spread over 12 months. This is a yearly, renewable contract, unless noted that the position is temporary. More information about faculty contract and work days is available here . Boise State University provides a best-in-class benefits package , including (but not limited to): 10.76% University contribution to your ORP retirement fund (Professional and Faculty employees) Excellent medical, dental and other health-related insurance coverages Tuition fee waiver benefits for employees, spouses and their dependents Dual Career Assistance Support for spouses and partners. See our full benefits page for more information About Boise State: Boise State University is a powerhouse of creativity, research, and impact. Our campus sits along the Boise River and reaches into the heart of Idaho’s capital – a vibrant hub for innovation, culture, and outdoor adventure. We’re nationally recognized for trailblazing research, student success, and community impact. Our faculty are scholars, mentors, and changemakers who advance ideas, discoveries, and the next generation of leaders. Boise offers the perfect backdrop – a welcoming city with a thriving arts scene, growing tech sector, and mountains on the horizon. Join us and help shape a bold, collaborative academic community. Learn more about faculty careers at Boise State and the City of Boise . Recommended Jobs Public Safety Officer Boise, Idaho Mid-Level, Safety and Security Director, Environmental Services Boise, Idaho Athletics, Environmental Safety, Mid-Level Assistant Producer, Idaho Matters Boise, Idaho Administration and Business Operations, Entry-Level, Marketing and Communications, Radio Equal Employment Opportunity Boise State University is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to all employees and qualified applicants for employment as provided for by federal, state, and local law. The university does not discriminate in employment opportunities or actions on the basis of any status or class protected under applicable federal, state, or local law. The University endeavors to recruit, hire, train, and promote the most qualified employees in all job titles. All other personnel actions are administered without regard to protected class under federal, state, or local law. If you require an accommodation at any step in our recruitment process, you are encouraged to contact (208) 426-1616 or hr@boisestate.edu. Jeanne Clery Act Each year, Boise State University prepares an Annual Security Report and Annual Fire Safety Report as part of the University's commitment to safety and security on campus and in compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Police and Crime Statistics Act. The report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on the main Boise State campus, all of Boise State's separate campuses, in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Boise State, and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from Boise State's campuses. Also included in the report are the University's institutional policies and procedures concerning campus security, including those related to sexual assault, campus alerts, drug and alcohol use, and others. You can obtain a copy of the report by accessing the following website or you can request a copy of the report by contacting the University's Department of Public Safety at 2245 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83706; by calling 208-426-6911; or by sending an email to publicsafety@boisestate.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Boise State University (208) 426-1000 1910 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83725 Facebook Instagram X Youtube LinkedIn Cookie Preferences Terms of Use Accessibility Privacy © 2026 All Rights Reserved. Boise State University Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
    },
    "https://jobs.boisestate.edu/jobs/assistant-or-associate-professor-in-quantitative-methods-boise-idaho-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor in Quantitative Methods",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor in Quantitative Methods - Boise, Idaho, United States Back to Boise State My Profile Kiosk mode Assistant or Associate Professor in Quantitative Methods Assistant or Associate Professor in Quantitative Methods 499944 Boise, Idaho Faculty Opening on: Oct 28 2026 The Department of Educational Leadership, Research, and Educational Technology (ELRT) at Boise State University seeks two tenure-track faculty members in Quantitative Methods at the rank of Assistant or Associate Professor. Successful candidates will contribute to quantitative research methods scholarship, including its application to education and/or public service. We are particularly interested in candidates with expertise in areas such as, but not limited to, evaluation, educational technology, learning sciences, teacher education, organizational learning, human resource development, education and public service leadership, curriculum and instruction, and public policy and administration. These are in-person, 9-month, tenure-track or tenured faculty positions starting in Fall 2027. The successful candidates will teach graduate courses on quantitative research methods, research design and methodology, program evaluation, educational measurement and assessment, and/or applied courses related to their area of expertise. Responsibilities include mentoring doctoral students (as an advisor or committee member), contributing to high-quality teaching and professional learning, and engaging in scholarly work that advances education and public service. The faculty members will also contribute to research and external funding, doctoral education, and program and professional service. Candidates with online teaching experience and experience supervising doctoral students are encouraged to apply. Department Overview: The ELRT department in Boise State University’s College of Education and Public Service is committed to preparing and supporting educators who serve the diverse needs of learners, schools, and communities. Through undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral programs, the department combines academic learning with meaningful practice, professional learning, research, and community partnerships. Faculty engage in teaching, doctoral education, clinical supervision, scholarship, service, and collaboration with P–12 schools, other disciplines, and community partners to strengthen educational practice and human performance. These efforts reflect Boise State’s broader commitments to student success, innovation, statewide impact, and meaningful community engagement. ELRT’s interconnected work prepares learners who are knowledgeable, reflective, collaborative, and committed to continued professional growth and to making a positive difference in the fields they serve. The College of Education and Public Service: The newly formed College of Education and Public Service (COEPS) at Boise State University empowers new generations of problem-solvers through expertise in leadership, learning, education, and public service. Its programs span undergraduate, graduate, and doctoral study and prepare students for careers in areas including teaching, educational leadership, educational technology, research, and workplace learning. COEPS faculty teach and conduct research across disciplines such as instructional design, educational technology, organizational development, leadership, and public service. The college serves diverse communities throughout Idaho and beyond, with particular attention to the needs of rural communities. Boise State’s location in Idaho’s capital gives faculty opportunities to engage in state-level discussions that inform educational and public policy in Idaho and beyond. Faculty work with undergraduate and graduate students, facilitate experiential learning, and advise and mentor students in the college’s four doctoral programs. COEPS and Boise State University are committed to providing resources that support faculty research, scholarship, teaching, and professional growth. As the university continues to grow, it welcomes talented faculty who want to contribute to its work and future. About Boise State: Boise State University is a powerhouse of creativity, research, and impact. Our campus sits along the Boise River and reaches into the heart of Idaho’s capital – a vibrant hub for innovation, culture, and outdoor adventure. We’re nationally recognized for trailblazing research, student success, and community impact. Our faculty are scholars, mentors, and changemakers who advance ideas, discoveries, and the next generation of leaders. Boise offers the perfect backdrop – a welcoming city with a thriving arts scene, growing tech sector, and mountains on the horizon. Join us and help shape a bold, collaborative academic community. Learn more about faculty careers at Boise State and the City of Boise . Minimum Qualifications: Earned doctoral degree in quantitative research methods, statistics, measurement and evaluation, educational research, curriculum and instruction, learning sciences, instructional design, educational foundations, or a related field. Candidates who are ABD may be considered if they complete the doctorate by the time of appointment. Demonstrated expertise in quantitative research methods and/or assessment and evaluation, including their application in education or a related field. Ability to teach graduate courses in quantitative research methodologies and methods at theoretical and/or applied levels. Commitment to advising graduate students. Evidence of scholarly impact, including peer-reviewed publications appropriate to the candidate’s career stage. Preferred Qualifications: Record of effective teaching of quantitative research methods as well as evaluation and measurement courses. Record of successful mentoring of graduate students, including chairing or serving on dissertation committees and guiding doctoral research projects. Experience applying quantitative methods to research in education, K-12 environments, public service, and/or a related field. Experience teaching in both traditional and online formats. Ability to provide effective student mentoring and foster a welcoming, engaging, and supportive learning environment. Evidence of grant-related experience or potential, including engagement with internal and/or external research funding appropriate to the candidate’s career stage. A record commensurate with appointment at the rank of advanced assistant or associate professor. Required Application Materials: A concise letter of application outlining the applicant’s qualifications, experience, and interest in the position, with specific reference to research, teaching, and service. Full C.V. One to three representative writing samples, which may be published or works in progress Contact information for three professional references Closing Date: Applications received by January 4, 2027 will receive full consideration. Review will begin January 5, 2027, and continue until the position is filled. For questions regarding this position or search process, please contact Dazhi Yang, Ph.D. at dazhiyang@boisestate.edu Salary and Benefits: The 9-month salary range for an Assistant Professor starts at $70,000; the range for an Associate Professor starts at $80,000. Salary will be commensurate with experience. Our standard faculty contract is a 9-month contract with pay and benefits spread over 12 months. This is a yearly, renewable contract. More information about faculty contracts and work days is available here . Boise State University provides a best-in-class benefits package , including (but not limited to): 10.76% University contribution to your ORP retirement fund (Professional and Faculty employees) Excellent medical, dental, and other health-related insurance coverages Tuition fee waiver benefits for employees, spouses and their dependents Dual Career Assistance Support for spouses and partners See our full benefits page for more information Recommended Jobs Public Safety Officer Boise, Idaho Mid-Level, Safety and Security Director, Environmental Services Boise, Idaho Athletics, Environmental Safety, Mid-Level Assistant Producer, Idaho Matters Boise, Idaho Administration and Business Operations, Entry-Level, Marketing and Communications, Radio Equal Employment Opportunity Boise State University is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to all employees and qualified applicants for employment as provided for by federal, state, and local law. The university does not discriminate in employment opportunities or actions on the basis of any status or class protected under applicable federal, state, or local law. The University endeavors to recruit, hire, train, and promote the most qualified employees in all job titles. All other personnel actions are administered without regard to protected class under federal, state, or local law. If you require an accommodation at any step in our recruitment process, you are encouraged to contact (208) 426-1616 or hr@boisestate.edu. Jeanne Clery Act Each year, Boise State University prepares an Annual Security Report and Annual Fire Safety Report as part of the University's commitment to safety and security on campus and in compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Police and Crime Statistics Act. The report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on the main Boise State campus, all of Boise State's separate campuses, in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Boise State, and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from Boise State's campuses. Also included in the report are the University's institutional policies and procedures concerning campus security, including those related to sexual assault, campus alerts, drug and alcohol use, and others. You can obtain a copy of the report by accessing the following website or you can request a copy of the report by contacting the University's Department of Public Safety at 2245 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83706; by calling 208-426-6911; or by sending an email to publicsafety@boisestate.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Boise State University (208) 426-1000 1910 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83725 Facebook Instagram X Youtube LinkedIn Cookie Preferences Terms of Use Accessibility Privacy © 2026 All Rights Reserved. Boise State University Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
