    "https://jobs.boisestate.edu/jobs/assistant-teaching-professor-in-cultural-and-applied-anthropology-boise-idaho-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Cultural and Applied Anthropology",
      "description": "Assistant Teaching Professor in Cultural and Applied Anthropology - Boise, Idaho, United States Back to Boise State My Profile Kiosk mode Assistant Teaching Professor in Cultural and Applied Anthropology Assistant Teaching Professor in Cultural and Applied Anthropology 499941 Boise, Idaho Faculty Opening on: Oct 27 2026 Closing on: Jan 5 2027 Boise State University’s Department of Anthropology invites applicants for a full-time Assistant Teaching Professor (non-tenure track) position. The position offers salary and promotion opportunities similar to those of tenure-track faculty, including advancement to associate and full teaching professor. We seek an Assistant Teaching Professor in Cultural and Applied Anthropology with a focus on User Experience Research . The workload assignment is approximately 80% teaching and 20% departmental service, which translates to the equivalent of four 3-credit classes each semester. Student mentoring is included in departmental service. The primary teaching duties will include teaching courses in our undergraduate and graduate user experience research certificates, as well as undergraduate cultural anthropology courses, aligned with department strengths. The ideal candidate will have demonstrated teaching experience in these areas. Department Overview: For more information on Anthropology department programs, students, mission, values, and goals, please visit our website: https://www.boisestate.edu/anthropology/ Minimum Qualification: Ph.D. in Anthropology by time of appointment Preferred Qualifications: Preference will be given to candidates with teaching and/or applied experience in user experience research. Required Application Materials: Applicants should submit: 1) A cover letter outlining your motivation for applying, teaching experience and related professional development, mentoring experience, and service to the discipline and institution; 2) a current curriculum vitae; and 3) the name and contact information for three references (letters will not be requested until later in the search process). Closing Date: The position will close for new applicants on January 5, 2027. For questions regarding this position, please contact Dr. John Ziker (jziker@boisestate.edu). Salary and Benefits: Starting salary is $62,000 - $64,000 Our standard faculty contract is a 9-month contract (unless otherwise noted) with pay and benefits spread over 12 months. This is a yearly, renewable contract. More information about faculty contract and work days is available here . Boise State University provides a comprehensive benefits package , including (but not limited to): 10.76% University contribution to your ORP retirement fund (Professional and Faculty employees) Excellent medical, dental and other health-related insurance coverages Tuition fee waiver benefits for employees, spouses and their dependents See our full benefits page for more information About the Department of Anthropology: The Department of Anthropology is dedicated to exploring the human experience across time and space. We passionately foster learning in anthropology, engage in fundamental and transformative research, and serve our community in confronting the problems faced by humankind and the planet. In doing so, we are building a new anthropology, one that bridges the subdisciplines within the field, connects anthropology to other disciplines, accelerates basic science toward transformative application, and integrates academic and non-academic worlds. At the heart of our department is a commitment to answering fundamental questions about what it means to be human. We explore how humans have evolved and adapted over time, how cultures and social relationships shape our lives, how people interact with their environments, and how communities respond to change and plan for the future. By examining the diversity of human experiences across cultures and through time, we help students develop a deeper understanding of the world and their place within it. Our department offers engaging undergraduate and graduate degree programs, along with professionally oriented programs in cultural resource management (CRM), user experience research (UX), and data analytics. Through the study of anthropology, students gain the perspective, practical skills, and curiosity to navigate an increasingly interconnected world and make meaningful contributions to their communities and professions, carrying forward our vision of an anthropology that bridges worlds. Recommended Jobs Public Safety Officer Boise, Idaho Mid-Level, Safety and Security Director, Environmental Services Boise, Idaho Athletics, Environmental Safety, Mid-Level Assistant Producer, Idaho Matters Boise, Idaho Administration and Business Operations, Entry-Level, Marketing and Communications, Radio Equal Employment Opportunity Boise State University is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to all employees and qualified applicants for employment as provided for by federal, state, and local law. The university does not discriminate in employment opportunities or actions on the basis of any status or class protected under applicable federal, state, or local law. The University endeavors to recruit, hire, train, and promote the most qualified employees in all job titles. All other personnel actions are administered without regard to protected class under federal, state, or local law. If you require an accommodation at any step in our recruitment process, you are encouraged to contact (208) 426-1616 or hr@boisestate.edu. Jeanne Clery Act Each year, Boise State University prepares an Annual Security Report and Annual Fire Safety Report as part of the University's commitment to safety and security on campus and in compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Police and Crime Statistics Act. The report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on the main Boise State campus, all of Boise State's separate campuses, in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Boise State, and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from Boise State's campuses. Also included in the report are the University's institutional policies and procedures concerning campus security, including those related to sexual assault, campus alerts, drug and alcohol use, and others. You can obtain a copy of the report by accessing the following website or you can request a copy of the report by contacting the University's Department of Public Safety at 2245 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83706; by calling 208-426-6911; or by sending an email to publicsafety@boisestate.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Boise State University (208) 426-1000 1910 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83725 Facebook Instagram X Youtube LinkedIn Cookie Preferences Terms of Use Accessibility Privacy © 2026 All Rights Reserved. Boise State University Candidate Details We use cookies Some are necessary to operate the website and its functions. Others help personalise, improve content and services to show you the most relevant job opportunities. With the decision \"Accept essential only\" we will respect your privacy and will not set cookies that aren't necessary for the operation of the site. Accept essential only Accept all"
