    "https://jobs.boisestate.edu/jobs/assistant-or-associate-professor-environmental-studies-boise-idaho-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant or Associate Professor, Environmental Studies",
      "description": "Assistant or Associate Professor, Environmental Studies - Boise, Idaho, United States Back to Boise State My Profile Kiosk mode Assistant or Associate Professor, Environmental Studies Assistant or Associate Professor, Environmental Studies 499915 Boise, Idaho Faculty Opening on: Oct 13 2026 The College of Education and Public Service (COEPS) seeks applicants for a tenure-track position at the Assistant or Associate Professor level, beginning in Fall 2027. The position will support the Environmental Studies , Master of Environmental Management , and Public Policy and Administration Ph.D. programs. Candidates should have research and teaching expertise in environmental policy, environmental governance, environmental politics, environmental system change, or a related area. Areas of particular interest include environmental and natural resource management; agricultural and food policy; energy policy; mining policy; science, technology, and society; and the intersection of global, environmental, and urban change. Competitive candidates will have an established record of social science research demonstrated through peer-reviewed publications and public-facing scholarship. The successful candidate will teach core courses in COEPS undergraduate and graduate programs, including the undergraduate Environmental Studies, Master of Environmental Management, and Public Policy and Administration Ph.D. programs. This position is a key hire to support the Public Policy and Administration Ph.D. program, and will include responsibility for mentoring and advising master’s and doctoral students. College and Program overview: The newly formed College of Education and Public Service (COEPS) empowers new generations of problem-solvers through expertise in leadership, education and public service. Its faculty teach and engage in research in a variety of disciplines, including public administration, environmental management, criminal justice, leadership and conflict management, political science, environmental studies, urban studies, global studies, and a variety of education disciplines. COEPS faculty work with students at both the undergraduate and graduate levels, facilitate experiential learning, and participate as advisors and mentors to students in the college’s three doctoral programs. The Environmental Studies program is an interdisciplinary degree program, employing a problem-based learning approach. The curriculum is rooted in concrete examples of environmental issues. Classes and internships incorporate hands-on and academic considerations of wildland fire, multicultural agriculture, foothills use-planning, urban restoration, and more. Faculty teaching in the Environmental Studies program also contribute to the Global Studies Program and Urban Studies & Community Development Program, where synergies align. The Master in Environmental Management (MEM) program is a shared program in the College of Education and Public Service and the School of Environment (SoE) within the College of Arts and Sciences. Faculty work in disciplines in the social sciences, natural sciences, and humanities, and many engage in interdisciplinary and transdisciplinary research, promoting understanding of social, ecological, and technical systems. The program prepares innovative and capable managers and researchers who understand the complexities of environmental challenges. The interdisciplinary Ph.D. in Public Policy and Administration prepares students for research and senior leadership roles in government, nonprofit organizations, and academia. About Boise State: Boise State University is a powerhouse of creativity, research, and impact. Our campus sits along the Boise River and reaches into the heart of Idaho’s capital – a vibrant hub for innovation, culture, and outdoor adventure. We’re nationally recognized for trailblazing research, student success, and community impact. At Boise State, we have a robust community of environmental scholars and practitioners, in both the social and natural sciences. Boise is also home to the National Interagency Fire Center, The World Center for Birds of Prey, and numerous environmentally oriented non-profit organizations. Our faculty are scholars, mentors, and changemakers who advance ideas, discoveries, and the next generation of leaders. Boise offers the perfect backdrop – a welcoming city with a thriving arts scene, growing tech sector, and mountains on the horizon. Join us and help shape a bold, collaborative academic community. Learn more about faculty careers at Boise State and the City of Boise . Minimum qualifications: Ph.D. in Environmental Studies, Environmental Science, Geography, Sociology, Anthropology or a related field at the time of appointment is required. Advanced ABD’s may be considered. Ability to teach core courses in the Environmental Studies program. Ability to teach courses in the Master of Environmental Management program. Ability to mentor and advise Ph.D. and MEM students. Preferred qualifications: Ability to teach graduate-level methods courses. Evidence of interdisciplinary scholarship, teaching, and engagement. Evidence of external grant activity. Experience or evidence of success in mentoring and advising doctoral students. Record commensurate with the rank of Advanced Assistant or Associate Professor. Required application materials: An updated curriculum vitae (CV). A letter of application outlining the applicant’s qualifications, experience, and interest in the position, with specific reference to research, teaching, and service. A sample of scholarly work. Contact information for three professional references. Closing Date: Position will remain open until filled, with review to begin on November 16, 2026. For questions regarding this position, please contact Dr. Nisha Bellinger, nishabellinger@boisestate.edu Salary and Benefits: The 9-month salary range for an Assistant Professor starts at $70,000; the range for an Associate Professor starts at $80,000. Salary will be commensurate with experience. Our standard faculty contract is a 9-month contract with pay and benefits spread over 12 months. This is a yearly, renewable contract. More information about faculty contracts and work days is available here . Boise State University provides a best-in-class benefits package , including (but not limited to): 10.76% University contribution to your ORP retirement fund (Professional and Faculty employees) Excellent medical, dental and other health-related insurance coverages Tuition fee waiver benefits for employees, spouses and their dependents Dual Career Assistance Support for spouses and partners See our full benefits page for more information Back to job search Apply Now First Name (required) 8dce088a Last Name (required) c5c3487f Email (required) fea48cbd Refer someone to this job Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Recommended Jobs Public Safety Officer Boise, Idaho Mid-Level, Safety and Security Director, Environmental Services Boise, Idaho Athletics, Environmental Safety, Mid-Level Assistant Producer, Idaho Matters Boise, Idaho Administration and Business Operations, Entry-Level, Marketing and Communications, Radio Equal Employment Opportunity Boise State University is committed to providing equal employment opportunities to all employees and qualified applicants for employment as provided for by federal, state, and local law. The university does not discriminate in employment opportunities or actions on the basis of any status or class protected under applicable federal, state, or local law. The University endeavors to recruit, hire, train, and promote the most qualified employees in all job titles. All other personnel actions are administered without regard to protected class under federal, state, or local law. If you require an accommodation at any step in our recruitment process, you are encouraged to contact (208) 426-1616 or hr@boisestate.edu. Jeanne Clery Act Each year, Boise State University prepares an Annual Security Report and Annual Fire Safety Report as part of the University's commitment to safety and security on campus and in compliance with the Jeanne Clery Disclosure of Campus Security Police and Crime Statistics Act. The report includes statistics for the previous three years concerning reported crimes that occurred on the main Boise State campus, all of Boise State's separate campuses, in certain off-campus buildings or property owned or controlled by Boise State, and on public property within, or immediately adjacent to and accessible from Boise State's campuses. Also included in the report are the University's institutional policies and procedures concerning campus security, including those related to sexual assault, campus alerts, drug and alcohol use, and others. You can obtain a copy of the report by accessing the following website or you can request a copy of the report by contacting the University's Department of Public Safety at 2245 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83706; by calling 208-426-6911; or by sending an email to publicsafety@boisestate.edu. Annual Security and Fire Safety Report Sign up for job alerts Job Alert 74eae9a7 Finalize your job alert by selecting criteria from the dropdowns below. You can select multiple options from each dropdown by returning to the combobox and re-entering the list of options. Submit at the end to create your job alert. Categories College of Education and Public Service College of Education and Public Service Employment Type Faculty Faculty Locations Boise, Idaho, United States Boise, Idaho, United States First Name (required) d43f4a6a Last Name (required) 4b2237dd Email (required) 3cb52480 Send Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Boise State University (208) 426-1000 1910 University Drive, Boise, Idaho 83725 Facebook Instagram X Youtube LinkedIn Cookie Preferences Terms of Use Accessibility Privacy © 2026 All Rights Reserved. Boise State University Candidate Details We use cookies